Unlike the namespace header, these values are in differing places depending on request (some inside `workflow_execution`, some top-level). Also, this will surely expand to standalone activities, schedules, etc and will need to apply to newer RPC calls.

I propose the approach be via proto annotations that simple code generators in Go, Java, TypeScript, and Rust can use to extract message. So it would look something like this in https://github.com/temporalio/api/blob/master/temporal/api/workflowservice/v1/service.proto:

```proto
    rpc RequestCancelWorkflowExecution (RequestCancelWorkflowExecutionRequest) returns (RequestCancelWorkflowExecutionResponse) {
        option (google.api.http) = {
            post: "/namespaces/{namespace}/workflows/{workflow_execution.workflow_id}/cancel"
            body: "*"
            additional_bindings {
                post: "/api/v1/namespaces/{namespace}/workflows/{workflow_execution.workflow_id}/cancel"
                body: "*"
            }
        };
        option (temporal.api.protometa.request-header) {
            header: "temporal-resource-id"
            value: "{workflow_execution.workflow_id}"
        };
    }
```

The code generators to make the interceptors needed to build this aren't that hard and this will give us future proofing.

Proto annotations is the best way to go about this. I would enforce that the option is always set and have a variant that opts out of this (e.g. for list APIs).

```proto3
        options (temporal.api.protometa.request-header) {
            not_set: true
        };
```
